findings.body:
**A planted procfs symlink makes the gate's slot read run without end**

Decision 96 says a file planted under the slots directory is "never blocked on", and that this keeps the gate's wait inside its bound with its signals live. `readPlainFile` checks only the file type and the size `fstat` reports, then reads to EOF:

```ts
const stat = fstatSync(fd);
if (!stat.isFile() || stat.size > PLAIN_FILE_MAX_BYTES) {
  throw new Error(`${path} is not a plain file`);
}
return readFileSync(fd, 'utf8');
```

The open has no `O_NOFOLLOW`. Linux procfs files are regular files that report `st_size` 0 and stream data. `/proc/self/pagemap` returns 8 bytes for each virtual page up to the task size (about 256 GiB on x86_64), and it resolves to the reading process, here the gate's own `pup`. `readFileSync(fd)` on Node 24 reads until EOF and ignores `st_size`. So the type and size checks both pass, and the read never ends in practice.

**Attack path.** A session runs `ln -s /proc/self/pagemap ~/.pupitre/slots/slot-0.lock`. The next `pup merge` on a Linux `PUP_SANDBOX=vm` host reaches `liveHolders` → `readRecord` → `readPlainFile` (`test-slot.service.ts` L119/L162), and `reclaim` reads the file again after the rename (L147). The read is synchronous, so `GATE_SLOT_WAIT_MS`, the SIGINT/SIGTERM listeners in `acquire` and every timer stop working. Memory grows until the process is OOM-killed, while the merge lock stays held. Workers' `pup session test` and the cap read in `pup launch` hit the same helper.

The function body moved here unchanged from `typescript.adapter.ts`. What this PR adds is the call sites that matter most (the gate's slot wait under the merge lock, and the cap read), plus decision 96's guarantee that they are safe.

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/1c26673e8f8871464fa7188136f0f18f3a8f62dc/src/adapters/capability.utils.ts#L14-L25

**Fix.** Bound the read itself rather than trusting `st_size`. Keep the `isFile()`/size pre-check as a fast reject, then read through `readSync`, which needs an import:

```ts
const buffer = Buffer.alloc(PLAIN_FILE_MAX_BYTES + 1);
let length = 0;
for (let read = -1; read !== 0 && length < buffer.length; length += read) {
  read = readSync(fd, buffer, length, buffer.length - length, null);
}
if (length > PLAIN_FILE_MAX_BYTES) throw new Error(`${path} is not a plain file`);
return buffer.toString('utf8', 0, length);
```

A pagemap link then fails after 1 MiB and reads as a dead holder's file, which `reclaim` removes. Adding `O_NOFOLLOW` for the slot, wait and cap reads would also close the symlink route.
